# be really into
/biː ˈrɪəli ˈɪntuː/
### być bardzo zainteresowanym czymś, jarać się czymś
### *A: I hear you're really into podcasts these days.*
### *B: Yeah, I listen to one every morning on the train.*

# be passionate about
/biː ˈpæʃənət əˈbaʊt/
### pasjonować się czymś
### *A: What made you apply for this job?*
### *B: I'm passionate about good design — always have been.*

# be a huge fan of
/biː ə hjuːdʒ fæn ɒv/
### być wielkim fanem czegoś
### *A: I'm a huge fan of working from home.*
### *B: Me too — I save two hours a day on the commute.*

# absolutely love
/ˈæbsəluːtli lʌv/
### uwielbiać
### *A: How was the new Thai place?*
### *B: We absolutely loved it — we're going back on Friday.*

# adore
/əˈdɔː/
### uwielbiać, przepadać za czymś
### *A: Anna, another coffee from that little café?*
### *B: I know, I know — I just adore the place.*

# can't get enough of
/kɑːnt ˌget ɪˈnʌf əv/
### nie móc się nasycić czymś
### *A: You're watching that series again?*
### *B: I can't get enough of it — don't judge me.*

# be quite fond of
/biː kwaɪt fɒnd ɒv/
### dość lubić coś, mieć sentyment do czegoś
### *A: Do you ever miss the old office?*
### *B: A little. I was quite fond of the view from the fifth floor.*

# enjoy
/ɪnˈdʒɔɪ/
### lubić, czerpać przyjemność z czegoś
### *A: Do you enjoy working in a team?*
### *B: I do — you learn so much from other people.*

# be keen on
/biː kiːn ɒn/
### lubić coś, być chętnym do czegoś
### *A: Is Tom coming hiking with us?*
### *B: I doubt it — he's not very keen on early mornings.*

# appreciate
/əˈpriːʃieɪt/
### doceniać, cenić sobie
### *A: I hope my comments weren't too direct.*
### *B: Not at all — I really appreciate honest feedback.*

# rather like
/ˈrɑːðə laɪk/
### całkiem lubić, dość lubić
### *A: Doesn't the rain bother you?*
### *B: Actually, I rather like walking in it.*

# have a soft spot for
/hæv ə ˈsɒft spɒt fɔː/
### mieć słabość do czegoś
### *A: Why do you always take the train to Kraków?*
### *B: I have a soft spot for old-fashioned train journeys.*

# get a kick out of
/ˌget ə ˈkɪk aʊt əv/
### czerpać frajdę z czegoś
### *A: Alita, another difficult customer? You look happy about it.*
### *B: What can I say — I get a kick out of solving tricky problems.*

# don't mind
/dəʊnt maɪnd/
### nie przeszkadza mi, nie mam nic przeciwko
### *A: Sorry, would you mind if we moved the meeting to five?*
### *B: I don't mind at all — five works fine.*

# not bothered by
/nɒt ˈbɒðəd baɪ/
### nie przejmować się czymś
### *A: Doesn't the noise in the open office drive you crazy?*
### *B: Honestly, I'm not bothered by it — I just put my headphones on.*

# can take it or leave it
/kən teɪk ɪt ɔː liːv ɪt/
### być obojętnym, wszystko jedno
### *A: Are you excited about the team-building day?*
### *B: To be honest, I can take it or leave it.*

# not fussed (about)
/nɒt ˈfʌst (əˈbaʊt)/
### nie robić sobie nic z czegoś, być obojętnym
### *A: Pizza or sushi tonight?*
### *B: I'm not fussed — whatever's easier.*

# be indifferent to
/biː ɪnˈdɪfrənt tuː/
### być obojętnym wobec czegoś
### *A: What did the team think of the new logo?*
### *B: Most people seem fairly indifferent to it, to be honest.*

# can't stand
/kɑːnt stænd/
### nie znosić
### *A: Why did you leave the meeting early?*
### *B: I can't stand it when people talk over each other.*

# absolutely loathe
/ˈæbsəluːtli ləʊð/
### nienawidzić, nie cierpieć
### *A: It's the end of the month — expense reports time.*
### *B: Don't remind me. I absolutely loathe filling those in.*

# can't bear
/kɑːnt beə/
### nie móc znieść
### *A: Could you turn the music down a bit?*
### *B: Sorry! I just can't bear working in total silence.*

# detest
/dɪˈtest/
### nie cierpieć, brzydzić się czymś
### *A: You're leaving at six in the morning? Why so early?*
### *B: I detest rush-hour traffic — I'd do anything to avoid it.*

# be fed up with
/biː ˌfed ˈʌp wɪð/
### mieć dość czegoś, mieć czegoś po dziurki w nosie
### *A: The lift's out of order again.*
### *B: Again?! I'm so fed up with this building.*

# have little patience for
/hæv ˌlɪtl ˈpeɪʃns fɔː/
### nie mieć cierpliwości do czegoś
### *A: Should we book an hour for the update?*
### *B: Let's keep it short — Anna has little patience for long meetings.*

# not a fan of
/nɒt ə fæn ɒv/
### nie przepadać za czymś
### *A: They've changed the schedule again for tomorrow.*
### *B: Of course they have. I'm really not a fan of last-minute changes.*

# not really keen on
/nɒt ˈrɪəli kiːn ɒn/
### raczej nie lubić czegoś
### *A: Are you coming to the networking event tonight?*
### *B: I'll show my face, but I'm not really keen on small talk.*

# don't care for
/dəʊnt keə fɔː/
### nie lubić, nie przepadać za czymś
### *A: I'll reply to the whole thread and copy everyone in.*
### *B: Could we just call instead? I don't care for long email chains.*

# put off by
/pʊt ɒf baɪ/
### zniechęcony czymś, zrażony do czegoś
### *A: Why did the candidate turn down our offer?*
### *B: Apparently she was put off by the lack of flexible hours.*

# have reservations about
/hæv ˌrezəˈveɪʃnz əˈbaʊt/
### mieć zastrzeżenia co do czegoś
### *A: So, can we confirm the new deadline?*
### *B: Not yet — the team still has reservations about it.*

# not my cup of tea
/nɒt maɪ ˌkʌp əv ˈtiː/
### nie w moim guście, nie dla mnie
### *A: Fancy coming to the karaoke night?*
### *B: Thanks, but singing in public is not my cup of tea.*

# prefer
/prɪˈfɜː/
### woleć, preferować
### *A: Should I send you the report, or shall we go through it together?*
### *B: I prefer to read it first, then we can talk.*

# 'd rather
/d ˈrɑːðə/
### wolałbym
### *A: We could take the motorway — it's faster.*
### *B: I'd rather take the scenic route, if we're not in a hurry.*

# 'd prefer to
/d prɪˈfɜː tuː/
### wolałbym (coś zrobić)
### *A: Shall we settle this over email?*
### *B: I'd prefer to discuss it in person, if that works for you.*

# prefer X to Y
/prɪˈfɜː tuː/
### woleć coś od czegoś
### *A: Are you flying to Berlin?*
### *B: No, I prefer trains to planes for short trips.*

# have no preference
/hæv nəʊ ˈprefrəns/
### nie mieć preferencji
### *A: Window or aisle seat?*
### *B: I have no preference — you choose.*

# 'd sooner
/d ˈsuːnə/
### wolałbym (raczej)
### *A: We could rush and catch the later flight.*
### *B: I'd sooner leave early and not have to run through the airport.*

# if it were up to me
/ɪf ɪt wɜːr ˌʌp tə ˈmiː/
### gdyby to zależało ode mnie
### *A: Another Monday morning meeting…*
### *B: If it were up to me, we'd never have meetings before ten.*


Dialogue 1 — Choosing how to work (workplace, neutral/business)
Anna: Before we set up the project schedule — do you prefer morning or afternoon check-ins?
Pete: Honestly, I have no preference. Both work for me.
Anna: Really? I'd rather get them done first thing. My brain switches off after lunch.
Pete: Fair enough. Mornings it is. Although I'd prefer to keep them short — fifteen minutes, tops.
Anna: Agreed. I have little patience for meetings that could have been an email.
Pete: Ha! You and me both.
Moves shown: asking about preference → deferring → stating preference with a reason → conceding + adding a condition → agreeing emphatically.

Dialogue 2 — The new café (social, informal)
Kat: Have you tried that new place on the corner? I absolutely love it.
Tom: The one with the green door? I went on Tuesday. I'm really into their coffee, actually.
Kat: Right? And the cakes! I can't get enough of the lemon one.
Tom: Hmm, lemon's not really my cup of tea. But their cheesecake — now that I could eat every day.
Kat: You should come Saturday. Sven's coming too — he gets a kick out of finding new places before everyone else does.
Tom: Sounds good. I'm in.
Moves shown: recommending → agreeing with a personal angle → enthusing → polite partial disagreement + counter-offer → extending an invitation (recycles V2 Ch2).

Dialogue 3 — Trains or planes? (travel, mixed register)
Sven: So, Berlin next month. Flying or taking the train?
Alita: I prefer trains to planes for anything under six hours. You can actually work on a train.
Sven: True. And I can't stand all the waiting around at airports. Security, boarding, delays…
Alita: Exactly. I'd sooner leave an hour earlier and travel in peace.
Sven: If it were up to me, the company would ban short-haul flights completely.
Alita: Careful — say that at the next meeting and they'll put you in charge of travel policy.
Sven: I wouldn't mind that, actually.
Moves shown: opening a decision → stating a comparative preference with a reason → agreeing + escalating → hypothetical opinion → humorous deflection → wry comeback. (B-line bonus: "wouldn't mind" stretches the Core item into a new pattern.)

Dialogue 4 — Feedback on the proposal (business, formal)
Alita: Thanks for reading the draft. Honest thoughts?
Pete: Overall it's strong. I really appreciate how clearly you've laid out the costs.
Alita: But…?
Pete: But I have reservations about the timeline. Three weeks feels tight.
Alita: I see. Would you say the team is put off by the deadline, or by the workload itself?
Pete: The deadline, mainly. I'd prefer to present this to the client with a five-week plan, if I'm honest.
Alita: That's fair. I'm not a fan of slipping dates either, but better now than halfway through.
Moves shown: inviting feedback → praise before criticism → prompting for the "but" → hedged criticism → clarifying question → softened counter-proposal ("if I'm honest") → conceding gracefully.

Dialogue 5 — Office gripes (workplace, informal/spoken)
Kat: Ugh. The lift's broken again. I am so fed up with this building.
Pete: Tell me about it. And I can't bear the new coffee machine. It takes five minutes per cup.
Kat: See, I don't mind the machine — it's the queue for it that kills me.
Pete: At least the new chairs are nice.
Kat: Honestly? I can take them or leave them. My old one was fine.
Pete: You're impossible to please. What do you like about this place?
Kat: The people, obviously. And Fridays. I'm a huge fan of Fridays.
Moves shown: venting → matching the complaint ("Tell me about it" — recycles V2 Ch7) → partial disagreement with a distinction → attempted positive turn → indifference → teasing challenge → warm resolution.
